    "question": {
      ":question/id": "6ece7f96-4fca-5116-a2f1-95bb30757786",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $N$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $N$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $det(N)=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$\n- **III.** $det(N)=- \\frac{5 \\sqrt{5}}{3}$\n\n$$\nN=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\sqrt{5} & -2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{5} & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(N) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{5} & -2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{5} & -6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & -6\\end{aligned}|+(- \\sqrt{5})|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & -1 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}|+(-2)|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(0+\\sqrt{5})+(- \\sqrt{5})(-2+2)+(-2)(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}+0) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\sqrt{5}+(- \\sqrt{5})\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(N)=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement II is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "28a54245-7101-5b72-9259-2be89ad42e90",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
