  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "3bc38489-322d-50cd-8ead-c929116f141f",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $E$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $E$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $det(E)=0$\n- **III.** $det(E)=- \\frac{1}{2}$\n\n$$\nE=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{7} & -2 \\\\ - \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{7} & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(E) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & \\sqrt{7} & -2 \\\\ - \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{7} & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{7} & 2\\end{aligned}|+(- \\sqrt{7})|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{1}{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-2)|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(0- \\sqrt{7})+(- \\sqrt{7})(-1+0)+(-2)(- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}+0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (- \\sqrt{7})+(- \\sqrt{7})\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot (- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}) \\\\  & =- 2 \\sqrt{7}+\\sqrt{7}+\\sqrt{7} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(E)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "c3bcc3c7-2350-57bc-a704-3efe08b288fd",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
