  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "02d1819b-aa7f-5344-8a06-92c67aa65b85",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $T$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $T$ is **not** a singular matrix\n\n- **II.** $T$ is a singular matrix\n\n- **III.** $det(T)=\\frac{10}{7}$\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & \\frac{1}{7} \\\\ 3 & 1 & \\frac{4}{7} \\\\ 2 & -2 & \\frac{2}{7}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & \\frac{1}{7} \\\\ 3 & 1 & \\frac{4}{7} \\\\ 2 & -2 & \\frac{2}{7}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{4}{7} \\\\ -2 & \\frac{2}{7}\\end{aligned}|+(-4)|\\begin{aligned}3 & \\frac{4}{7} \\\\ 2 & \\frac{2}{7}\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{1}{7}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(\\frac{2}{7}+\\frac{8}{7})+(-4)(\\frac{6}{7}- \\frac{8}{7})+\\frac{1}{7}(-6-2) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{7}+\\frac{8}{7})+(-4)\\cdot (- \\frac{2}{7})+\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot (-8) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(T)=\\frac{10}{7}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "e2209a0b-1401-5749-9a5a-d3afd2af0bbe",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
