    "question": {
      ":question/id": "7c648dc9-5d67-5fa3-9e01-a21b5bd8feda",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $D$ is **not** a singular matrix\n\n- **II.** $D$ is a singular matrix\n\n- **III.** $det(D)=1$\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & - \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & - \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & - \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & - \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(1+2)+3(- \\frac{3}{2}+0)+1(3+0) \\\\  & =(1+2)+3\\cdot (- \\frac{3}{2})+(3+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=\\frac{3}{2}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement I is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "e3992686-f07d-5bc6-8085-07c96c52d8cf",
