    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "772dd18f-b1bc-58d9-9384-ecbd2abd30e1",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $P$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $P$ is **not** a singular matrix\n\n- **II.** $P$ is a singular matrix\n\n- **III.** $det(P)=- \\sqrt{2}$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2} & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 2 & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2} & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 2 & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & -1 \\\\ 2 & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}|+(-1)|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-2+2)+(-1)(- \\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2})+0 \\\\  & =(-2+2)+(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(P)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement II is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
