  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "169ac0b5-6ffb-546d-9f29-227316ba3231",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $P$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(P)=0$\n- **II.** $det(P)=- 2 \\sqrt{3}$\n- **III.** $P$ is a singular matrix\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 \\sqrt{3} & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 \\sqrt{3} & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 \\sqrt{3} & 4\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{1}{3}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 \\sqrt{3} & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(8-6)+0+\\frac{1}{3}(0- 6 \\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 2+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (- 6 \\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =2 \\sqrt{3}- 2 \\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(P)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "d11ae58d-3a64-5f46-a6b4-0cb2878f01cf",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
