  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "3ed22a59-ec22-5311-87df-c2a3fab85a35",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $N$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $N$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **II.** $N$ is a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(N)=4$\n\n$$\nN=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -2 & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(N) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -2 & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ -2 & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}|+(-2)|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}-4 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-2+4)+(-2)(4 \\sqrt{2}- 2 \\sqrt{2})+0 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 2 \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =4- 4 \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =4 - 4 \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(N)=4 - 4 \\sqrt{2}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement I is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "2e8a4b1b-cc7d-5d9f-b76b-3e1809d8c814",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
