    "question": {
      ":question/id": "00f78f87-40dd-5d29-97dc-466ed31a2464",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $N$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $N$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **II.** $N$ is a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(N)=\\sqrt{5}$\n\n$$\nN=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & -1 & 4 \\\\ - 3 \\sqrt{5} & -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(N) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & -1 & 4 \\\\ - 3 \\sqrt{5} & -1 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ - 3 \\sqrt{5} & 5\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{1}{3}|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ - 3 \\sqrt{5} & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}(-5+4)+0+\\frac{1}{3}(0- 3 \\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot (-1)+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (- 3 \\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =- \\sqrt{5}- \\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =- 2 \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(N)=- 2 \\sqrt{5}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement I is true.",
