    "question": {
      ":question/id": "c1375150-b9be-5660-8321-caa9d068f7c3",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(D)=- \\frac{21}{2}$\n- **II.** $det(D)=- \\frac{15}{2}$\n- **III.** $D$ is a singular matrix\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & - \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -3 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & - \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -3 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)|\\begin{aligned}-1 & - \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}-1 & - \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)(0- \\frac{3}{2})+2(0- \\frac{9}{2})+0 \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (- \\frac{3}{2})+2\\cdot (- \\frac{9}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-9 \\\\  & =- \\frac{15}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=- \\frac{15}{2}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement II is true.",
