    "question": {
      ":question/id": "ee5bb660-382f-5d85-91d1-b36f301ce9b8",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $T$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $T$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **II.** $T$ is a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(T)=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3} & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3} & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & -2\\end{aligned}|+(- \\sqrt{3})|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)(0+\\sqrt{3})+(- \\sqrt{3})(-1+0)+1(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+0) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\sqrt{3}+(- \\sqrt{3})\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =- 2 \\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(T)=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
