  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "db7d133f-3ec8-5692-93ce-82d2924bfe8b",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $N$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $N$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **II.** $N$ is a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(N)=4 - 4 \\sqrt{3}$\n\n$$\nN=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 4 \\sqrt{3} & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(N) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 4 \\sqrt{3} & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 4 \\sqrt{3} & 4\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{1}{2}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 4 \\sqrt{3} & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(8-4)+0+\\frac{1}{2}(0- 8 \\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 4+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (- 8 \\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =4 \\sqrt{3}- 4 \\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(N)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement II is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "298743f5-2710-5eb4-b1ae-b0684538f2ba",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
