  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "dc1a36b5-4727-5e41-9baa-e592509e2486",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $N$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $N$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **II.** $N$ is a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(N)=\\frac{16}{5}$\n\n$$\nN=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5} & - \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(N) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5} & - \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{aligned}|+(- \\frac{1}{5})|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}|+(- \\frac{3}{5})|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(-6-6)+(- \\frac{1}{5})(-3+4)+(- \\frac{3}{5})(-3-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot (-12)+(- \\frac{1}{5})\\cdot 1+(- \\frac{3}{5})\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =- \\frac{36}{5}- \\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{21}{5} \\\\  & =- \\frac{16}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(N)=- \\frac{16}{5}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement I is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "e93b4258-82dc-5948-9623-7dc411919fd6",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
