    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "4db171c1-9bda-5ad9-a883-7576e9b1522a",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $P$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $P$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $P$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(P)=\\frac{13}{2}$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & \\frac{3}{2} & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & \\frac{3}{2} & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & 4\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)(4- \\frac{3}{2})+0+1(\\frac{9}{2}+2) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}+1\\cdot \\frac{13}{2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{13}{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(P)=4\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement II is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "18ed3e32-c1c7-5a5f-8785-f093cf408324",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
