    "question": {
      ":question/id": "a081e507-86b8-5bf0-a015-2c9e77f74455",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $M$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $M$ is **not** a singular matrix\n\n- **II.** $M$ is a singular matrix\n\n- **III.** $det(M)=-9$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ -3 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ -3 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}|+(- \\frac{1}{3})|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{4}{3}|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(3+12)+(- \\frac{1}{3})(9+0)+\\frac{4}{3}(-12+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 15+(- \\frac{1}{3})\\cdot 9+\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(M)=-9\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
